1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,500
Open-ended questions are those that seek information or open up areas for thought and discussion.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:11,500
They're often defined as questions that can't be answered by yes, no, or maybe, and, certainly,

3
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:16,000
they are not rhetorical or testing questions that have right and wrong answers.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Open-ended questions influence others in two very different ways.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:26,000
At the simplest level, they provide information that can be used to make other influence behaviors more effective.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,500
For example, by identifying needs, goals, or decision criteria.

7
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:37,000
At a more advanced level, these questions stimulate the other person to think and talk about topics of mutual interest

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,500
that may not have occurred to him or her otherwise.

9
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:44,000
In this way, the behavior promotes creative thinking as well as information sharing,

10
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:48,000
and is thus essential to team innovation processes.

11
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Here are some examples.

